WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.722
This paper introduces First-order Repulsive Deep
Ensembles, or FoRDE,

00:00:04.802 --> 00:00:07.466
a new way to train neural network ensembles.

00:00:07.546 --> 00:00:11.893
Deep ensembles work well because their members
learn diverse functions,

00:00:11.973 --> 00:00:17.380
but existing methods that repel members in weight
space or function space have struggled

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.563
to improve on plain deep ensembles.

00:00:19.643 --> 00:00:25.362
The key idea here is to instead repel ensemble
members in the space of their input gradients.

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:32.346
Because input gradients uniquely characterize a
function up to translation and are far smaller than the weights,

00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:39.142
this guarantees members become functionally
different and encourages each network to learn distinct features.

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:45.440
The result is an ensemble that is markedly more
robust and better calibrated under input corruptions.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:52.668
Ensembles of neural networks are powerful because
different members capture different explanations of the data.

00:00:52.748 --> 00:00:58.036
Particle-based variational inference tries to make
this diversity explicit by adding a

00:00:58.116 --> 00:01:00.595
repulsion term that pushes members apart.

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:03.279
But where you apply that repulsion matters.

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.651
Repelling in weight space is wasteful,

00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:12.330
because neural networks are heavily
over-parameterized and many different weights encode the same function.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.700
Repelling directly in function space sounds appealing,

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.319
but it requires comparing entire functions, which
is computationally hard,

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:23.877
and the shortcuts used in prior work led to underfitting.

00:01:23.957 --> 00:01:30.930
So neither weight-space nor function-space
repulsion had delivered meaningful gains over standard deep ensembles.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.785
The authors take a third view of a neural network.

00:01:34.865 --> 00:01:38.341
Beyond its weights and its function values, a model
can be represented,

00:01:38.421 --> 00:01:41.896
up to a translation, by its first-order input
gradients, that is,

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:45.452
the derivatives of the output with respect to the input.

00:01:45.532 --> 00:01:49.007
This representation has two attractive properties.

00:01:49.087 --> 00:01:52.562
First, input gradients are the same size as the input,

00:01:52.642 --> 00:01:56.118
which is far smaller than the enormous weight vector,

00:01:56.198 --> 00:01:59.673
so they are much cheaper to compare with a kernel.

00:01:59.753 --> 00:02:03.228
Second,

00:02:03.308 --> 00:02:06.784
forcing members to have different input gradients
means forcing them to depend on different input features.

00:02:06.864 --> 00:02:10.339
Intuitively, this should make the ensemble more robust,

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.895
because if members react to complementary patterns,

00:02:13.975 --> 00:02:17.450
corrupting one pattern will not fool all of them at once.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.916
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:21.996 --> 00:02:26.081
First, it introduces First-order Repulsive Deep Ensembles,

00:02:26.161 --> 00:02:30.247
a method that adds a repulsion term defined on
input gradients rather than weights or function outputs.

00:02:30.327 --> 00:02:34.412
Second,

00:02:34.492 --> 00:02:38.578
it develops a practical kernel that compares the
normalized input gradients of the true label across training data,

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:42.743
keeping computation linear in the number of samples.

00:02:42.823 --> 00:02:46.909
Third,

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:51.074
it proposes a principled way to choose the kernel
lengthscales using the principal components of the data,

00:02:51.154 --> 00:02:55.240
which lets FoRDE emphasize high-variance features
and become especially robust to input corruptions.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.292
FoRDE is built on Wasserstein gradient descent,

00:02:59.372 --> 00:03:03.044
a particle-based variational inference method.

00:03:03.124 --> 00:03:06.796
Each network in the ensemble is a particle, and its
update has two terms.

00:03:06.876 --> 00:03:10.548
The driving force pulls the particle toward
high-density regions of the Bayesian posterior,

00:03:10.628 --> 00:03:14.300
just like ordinary training.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.052
The repulsion force, weighted by a kernel, pushes
particles apart.

00:03:18.132 --> 00:03:21.804
The crucial design choice is the kernel.

00:03:21.884 --> 00:03:25.556
Instead of comparing weights or outputs,

00:03:25.636 --> 00:03:29.308
FoRDE compares the input gradients of the true
label, normalized to the unit sphere,

00:03:29.388 --> 00:03:33.060
using a radial basis function kernel.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:36.812
The gradients are normalized because their
magnitude shrinks as training converges,

00:03:36.892 --> 00:03:40.564
and comparing directions teaches members to rely on
complementary features.

00:03:40.644 --> 00:03:44.316
Finally,

00:03:44.396 --> 00:03:48.068
the lengthscales of the kernel are chosen from the
principal components of the data,

00:03:48.148 --> 00:03:51.820
so repulsion is strongest along the most
informative input directions.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.852
The method is tested across a broad range of settings.

00:03:55.932 --> 00:04:01.726
Illustrative one-dimensional regression and
two-dimensional classification tasks show

00:04:01.806 --> 00:04:06.563
how input-gradient repulsion increases uncertainty
away from the data.

00:04:06.643 --> 00:04:12.368
The main image classification experiments use
CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and TinyImageNet,

00:04:12.448 --> 00:04:17.206
with ResNet and PreActResNet backbones and an
ensemble of ten members.

00:04:17.286 --> 00:04:22.389
To measure robustness, the authors use the
corrupted benchmarks CIFAR-10-C,

00:04:22.469 --> 00:04:24.600
CIFAR-100-C, and TinyImageNet-C,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.507
which apply nineteen types of image corruption at
five severity levels.

00:04:29.587 --> 00:04:34.897
They report accuracy, negative log-likelihood, and
expected calibration error,

00:04:34.977 --> 00:04:38.560
both on clean data and averaged over all corruptions.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.280
Across the corrupted image benchmarks,

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.610
FoRDE with PCA lengthscales is the strongest method
on every metric,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.412
while remaining competitive on clean images.

00:04:48.492 --> 00:04:50.640
Compared to the second-best method,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:58.027
it improves accuracy by about two point four
percent on CIFAR-10-C and about one point three percent on CIFAR-100-C.

00:04:58.107 --> 00:05:02.548
The toy experiments tell a consistent story: in one
and two dimensions,

00:05:02.628 --> 00:05:08.279
FoRDE places higher uncertainty in regions away
from the training data than deep ensembles

00:05:08.359 --> 00:05:10.062
and other repulsive methods,

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:15.920
which is direct evidence that repelling input
gradients yields greater functional diversity.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.567
The paper carefully ablates the kernel lengthscales,

00:05:19.647 --> 00:05:23.814
which govern how repulsion is distributed across
input dimensions.

00:05:23.894 --> 00:05:27.740
The PCA lengthscales give the best robustness to
corruptions,

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.701
because they emphasize high-variance features.

00:05:30.781 --> 00:05:37.909
The identity lengthscales instead give the best
clean accuracy on CIFAR-100 and the best likelihood on CIFAR-10,

00:05:37.989 --> 00:05:39.840
but sacrifice some robustness.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.796
Tuning between the two extremes yields the best of
both worlds in most cases.

00:05:44.876 --> 00:05:50.524
A separate study varies the ensemble size and finds
that a FoRDE with ten members matches

00:05:50.604 --> 00:05:55.415
or exceeds the corruption robustness of a deep
ensemble with thirty members,

00:05:55.495 --> 00:05:58.440
showing that the diversity gain is substantial.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:03.063
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:03.143 --> 00:06:07.386
Under input corruptions,

00:06:07.466 --> 00:06:11.709
FoRDE with PCA lengthscales improves accuracy by
about two point four percent on CIFAR-10-C

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:16.031
and one point three percent on CIFAR-100-C over the
next-best method.

00:06:16.111 --> 00:06:20.354
Its diversity is efficient too: a ten-member FoRDE
reaches the same corruption robustness

00:06:20.434 --> 00:06:24.677
as a thirty-member deep ensemble.

00:06:24.757 --> 00:06:29.000
And these gains hold across a demanding benchmark
of nineteen corruption types at five severity levels each.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:35.850
The lasting message of this paper is that the space
in which you enforce diversity matters as much as the amount.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:40.256
By repelling ensemble members in the compact space
of their input gradients,

00:06:40.336 --> 00:06:46.226
FoRDE guarantees that members become genuinely
different functions that rely on complementary features,

00:06:46.306 --> 00:06:51.675
without the waste of weight-space repulsion or the
intractability of function-space repulsion.

00:06:51.755 --> 00:06:55.906
Combined with data-driven lengthscales from
principal component analysis,

00:06:55.986 --> 00:07:01.065
this yields ensembles that are more robust and
better calibrated under the kinds of input

00:07:01.145 --> 00:07:03.500
corruptions that matter in the real world.
